            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-11731",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "libxslt: Type Confusion in exsltFuncResultCompfunction of libxslt"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A flaw was found in the exsltFuncResultComp() function of libxslt, which handles EXSLT \u003cfunc:result\u003e elements during stylesheet parsing. Due to improper type handling, the function may treat an XML document node as a regular XML element node, resulting in a type confusion. This can cause unexpected memory reads and potential crashes. While difficult to exploit, the flaw could lead to application instability or denial of service."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-11731",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-11731\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: libxslt1.1\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2025-11731](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-11731)\nA flaw was found in the exsltFuncResultComp() function of libxslt, which handles EXSLT \u003cfunc:result\u003e elements during stylesheet parsing. Due to improper type handling, the function may treat an XML document node as a regular XML element node, resulting in a type confusion. This can cause unexpected memory reads and potential crashes. While difficult to exploit, the flaw could lead to application instability or denial of service.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-11731**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|libxslt1.1||[CVE-2025-11731](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-11731)|\n\nA flaw was found in the exsltFuncResultComp() function of libxslt, which handles EXSLT \u003cfunc:result\u003e elements during stylesheet parsing. Due to improper type handling, the function may treat an XML document node as a regular XML element node, resulting in a type confusion. This can cause unexpected memory reads and potential crashes. While difficult to exploit, the flaw could lead to application instability or denial of service."
